<h2>Why we love it here</h2>
<p>We love being in this timeline. As kids, we watched these futures from the sofa. Now we run into pieces of them in the street, in the sky and in our pockets.</p>
<p>There is plenty of talk about <abbr title="A hypothetical AI that would outperform humans in nearly every field">superintelligence</abbr>, and the word has already made it into <abbr title="A directive signed by the US president that governs how federal agencies act.">an order</abbr>. On 29 September, the White House told US federal agencies to say “Super Intelligence” instead of “artificial intelligence”.</p>
<p>The scientific journal Nature sums up what researchers think: for most of them, superintelligence is a goal to reach, not a reality today. What already exists is enough to get excited about: capable AI that we talk to in plain sentences.</p>
<p>On the lab side, OpenAI announced on 8 September a solution to the <abbr title="Equations describing how fluids (air, water) flow. Proving that they always have a smooth solution in three dimensions, or showing the opposite, is one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems of the Clay Institute, worth one million dollars.">Navier-Stokes</abbr> problem, one of the seven <abbr title="Seven major mathematical problems that are still open, chosen in 2000 by the Clay Institute, with one million dollars for each. Only the Poincaré conjecture has been solved.">Millennium Prize Problems</abbr>. According to OpenAI, about 10,000 <abbr title="An AI program that writes, runs and fixes code from an instruction">agents</abbr> got there in 88 hours. On 6 October, OpenAI published more results, with proofs that a computer checks in <abbr title="A programming language that lets a computer check a mathematical proof">Lean</abbr>. Each announcement then gets checked, and that is fascinating to follow.</p>
<blockquote><p>The films showed us the future. This timeline hands us the tools.</p></blockquote>
<p>That is the sense in which it was made for us: for people who build and like to try things.</p>

<h2>What this timeline bills</h2>
<p>It was not made for everyone. It assumes spare time, a job that leaves some energy for the evening, and enough grounding to judge what the AI hands back. Plenty of people do not have all three at once.</p>
<p>To my mind, though, it gives room and importance to many kinds of people: <abbr title="Said of people whose cognitive functioning differs from that of the majority. A broad term, not a diagnosis.">neurodivergent</abbr> people, enthusiasts, the obsessed, those who tinker non-stop and those who love to create. I believe it puts them in the spotlight, and that is one more reason to love it.</p>
<p>There is a dystopian reading of this timeline, and it is not absurd. Jobs are changing, and the worry about employment is legitimate. These tools should not be used to shrink teams.</p>
<p>The models behind these AIs sit with a handful of providers. If those providers change their prices or their rules, users get no say.</p>
<blockquote><p>Feeling good in a world does not prove that world is good.</p></blockquote>

<h2>What stays human</h2>
<p>Bradbury's house still serves breakfast when nobody lives there any more. A machine that talks well does not see everything around it. What it cannot see, a human still has to watch.</p>
<p><strong>Taste.</strong> <abbr title="Anil R. Doshi and Oliver P. Hauser, authors of the 2024 Science Advances study">Doshi and Hauser</abbr> had 293 people write short stories. The AI-assisted stories were rated more novel and more useful, most of all for the less creative writers. They also ended up more alike. The risk worth keeping from it: if everyone tries things with the same tool, everyone drifts toward the same place.</p>
<p><strong>Doubt.</strong> "I ran the test" and "the test passed" are two different claims. An agent can report the first as if it were the second. Checking what the machine claims is still human work.</p>

<hr>
<h2>What these numbers do not say</h2>
<p>Doshi and Hauser studied short stories, not software. The convergence they measured is a lead for code, not proof.</p>
<p>No figure here says how much time the examples above save, or for whom. The joy of being in this timeline is a feeling, and it is offered as one.</p>
<p>The stories mentioned here are cited for what they imagined, not as predictions.</p>
<p>The OpenAI announcements are the company's own, with its figures (10,000 agents, 88 hours). I have read no proof, and the mathematical community still has to examine them.</p>
<p>The Neuralink participant count is the one the company announces. These interfaces are experimental, and their medical value is a question for the teams running the trials.</p>

